<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Darwin is a 40-year-old man with a provisional diagnosis of cyclic vomiting syndrome, on amitriptyline for five weeks and at 75 mg at bedtime for the past week, who comes for a same-day visit. Three days ago he developed a sore throat, then a runny and blocked nose and a dry cough. His temperature at home was 37.9 °C last night. He has had no nausea, sweating, trembling, or other sign of his usual prodrome. He has used the abortive medicines once, for an episode two weeks into treatment that settled in about ten hours, and has had no episode since. He is eating and drinking normally and passing urine as usual. He has no breathlessness, chest pain, ear or facial pain, or rash. He asked which cold medicines are safe with his new prescriptions, because infections have brought on episodes before and he wants to sleep through the cough. He has taken only honey and lemon so far. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Sore throat, runny and blocked nose, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild; cough disturbs sleep</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Cyclic vomiting syndrome on amitriptyline 75 mg; infections have triggered episodes before</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Honey and lemon help the throat</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Temperature to 37.9 °C; no prodrome, nausea, vomiting, breathlessness, or chest pain</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

