<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Common cold (J00)</content>. Viral upper respiratory infection with a clear chest and normal oxygen saturation. Cough is present and there is no tonsillar exudate, so streptococcal pharyngitis is unlikely and no throat test or antibiotic is needed. [S]<list><item>Honey in warm drinks, fluids, saline nasal spray, and rest.</item><item>Acetaminophen 1 g up to every 6 hours as needed for fever or sore throat, no more than 3 g a day.</item><item>Avoid nighttime cold and sleep products containing diphenhydramine, doxylamine, or chlorpheniramine, which add drowsiness, dry mouth, and constipation to amitriptyline.</item><item>Avoid cough products containing dextromethorphan while on amitriptyline and sumatriptan, and avoid oral decongestants such as pseudoephedrine, which can raise the pulse.</item><item>The cough may last two to three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cyclic vomiting syndrome, provisional (R11.15)</content>. No prodrome so far. Infection, fever, and lost sleep are recognized triggers, so the abortive plan should be at hand for the next few days. [S]<list><item>Continue amitriptyline 75 mg at bedtime.</item><item>At the first sign of the prodrome, take sumatriptan 20 mg nasal spray and ondansetron 8 mg dissolving tablet together, lie in a dark quiet room, and sip fluids.</item><item>Keep regular small meals and as much sleep as the cough allows.</item><item>Use the emergency department letter if vomiting continues for more than six hours despite the abortive medicines, or if he cannot keep fluids down.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Guidelines on management of cyclic vomiting syndrome in adults by the American Neurogastroenterology and Motility Society and the Cyclic Vomiting Syndrome Association (2019); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Message the clinic if the cold brings on an episode. Three-month review as planned in three weeks, with the ECG at the target dose. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-09: three-month review: amitriptyline at target dose, ECG, episode diary, and anxiety screen. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
